#!/usr/bin/python3
"""homelab-reconcile: bring this host's units and legacy entries in line with its packages.

The design is H3's *The reconcile unit* (docs/archive/host/h3-pi-pilot.md), and the
step numbers below are that section's. `homelab-reconcile.service` runs this as root, under the
host's /usr/bin/python3, which is 3.12 on Ubuntu 24.04. It uses the standard library only, and
hosts/lib/tests/test_reconcile_component.py parses it with the 3.12 grammar. The activation step,
the four arms and the metrics file live in the reconcile_lib package beside it, which the same
component ships; the reconcile-size census counts both against one line ceiling.

Usage: homelab-reconcile        (exit 0 when every step succeeded, 1 otherwise)
       homelab-reconcile --metrics   (rewrite the metrics file from the record; acts on nothing)
       homelab-reconcile --wait-for-apt   (block until dpkg's frontend lock is free; exit 0)

`--metrics` is what `homelab-reconcile-metrics.service` runs at boot, so the file stops reading
`homelab_host_reboot_pending 1` once the boot has cleared the flag (#6188). It takes no lock and
writes no record. It drops each pending unit that has restarted since, as a full run would.

It takes dpkg's frontend lock with a blocking lockf, so a start from a postinst waits until the
whole apt transaction has finished. Under the lock it reads the manifest, its own state and both
packages' status, then releases the lock before its first systemctl call.

`--wait-for-apt` is what `homelab-reconcile-wait.service` runs. homelab-reconcile.service wants
that unit and is ordered after it, so the wait for apt's post-invoke hooks spends the wait
unit's start timeout, not reconcile's (#7648). Its journal line is `wait for apt to release the
dpkg lock`, and a full run's own `wait for the dpkg lock` then reads near zero. The wait unit has
no enable link and is inactive once it exits, so no reconcile step starts or restarts it.

The manifest, /usr/lib/homelab/manifest.json, which the host package ships:

    {"units": {"<unit>": {"policy": "manual" | "windowed"}, "<unit>": {}, ...},
     "enable_links": ["<unit>", ...],
     "legacy": [{"path": "/abs/path"}, {"cron": "<name>", "user": "<user>"},
                {"cron_file": "<name under /etc/cron.d>"}, {"unit": "<Ansible-era unit>"}],
     "handoffs": [{"units": ["<unit>", ...], "legacy": [<legacy entry>, ...]}, ...],
     "reconcilers": [] | ["libvirt", "lines", "ufw", "users"],
     "activations": {"<packaged path>": "<action>"},
     "ufw": {"rules": [...], "legacy": [...]}, "libvirt": {...}, "users": {...},
     "lines": {...},
     "secrets_inputs_sha256": "<hex>"}

`units` names every unit reconcile manages, another homelab package's and another package's
drop-in target included. `enable_links` names each unit whose enable link the manifest's
packages ship. `handoffs` pairs each spec's legacy entries with the units that spec ships, and
step 4 keeps an entry while one of those units failed to start in this run, or its step 3b
activation call failed. `reconcilers` lists
the arms the manifest carries state for. The four arms are in
ARMS, and each section's schema is the docstring of its reconcile_lib module, so a manifest
listing another kind fails the run here. The udev arm (H4.3b1) is not one of them: it hashes the
package's /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/NN-homelab-*.rules files, as reconcile_lib/udev.py says.
`activations` maps a
packaged file to the action step 3b runs when its hash changes (reconcile_lib/activate.py).
`secrets_inputs_sha256` is present only when the package ships the `secrets-unit` component, and
every secrets rule below keys on it.

The restart contract (docs/archive/host/h4-secrets-unit.md, *The restart contract*):
reconcile is the only process that starts or restarts a unit outside the boot path. It never starts
or restarts a `windowed` unit, and never restarts a `manual` one. Step 1b runs `homelab-secrets`
when the secrets inputs changed. The restart set, built before step 2, holds the units whose file
or drop-in changed and the units in /run/homelab-secrets/pending.json, each only when it is
enabled or active. Step 2 starts the enabled units outside the set, and step 3 restarts the set.
A unit gets at most one call per run. `reset-failed` precedes it, and no call uses `--no-block`.
A unit whose outputs do not exist, or whose call fails, joins pending.json and keeps its old hash,
and the run exits 1. An entry leaves pending.json after reconcile acts on it, or when its unit is
`windowed`, `manual`, masked, or neither enabled nor active.

Its state and record, /var/lib/homelab-reconcile/applied.json:

    {"result":  {"versions": {"<package>": "<version>"}, "ok": <bool>, "finished": <epoch>},
     "last_ok": {the same fields, from the newest run that succeeded},
     "units": {"<unit>": {"file": "<sha256>", "dropin": "<sha256>"} | null},
     "enable_links": ["<unit>", ...],
     "retired": ["<legacy entry as canonical JSON>", ...],
     "files": {"<packaged path>": "<sha256>"},     (activated files, by the hash that ran)
     "pending": {"<unit>": "<InvocationID>"},      (units owed a restart: the manual: actions)
     "started": {"<unit>": <epoch>},               (when steps 2, 3 or 4b last called the unit)
     "drift": {"<arm>": <items the arm left alone>},
     "ufw": {"ids": ["<rule id>", ...]},           (once any manifest has declared ufw)
     "users": {"names": [...], "expired": [...]}, "libvirt": {"networks": {...}, "nwfilters": {...}},  (likewise)
     "udev": {"<rule path>": "<sha256>", ...},     (only while a rule is shipped)
     "secrets_inputs_sha256": "<hex>"}   (only after a run in which homelab-secrets succeeded)

homelab-updater reads only `result` and `last_ok`. Each per-entry item advances only when its
step succeeded on it, or when the step's rule left it alone, so the next run retries exactly
the actions that failed. A `null` hash reads as changed.

Drift is an item an arm or a rule left alone on purpose: a ufw rule the floor kept, a libvirt
object a running guest still references, a user the floor kept, or a legacy service that is still
running. It is counted in `drift`, never fails the run, and is retried by
the next run. A fault fails the run, which holds the host. The run also writes
homelab_host_reconcile.prom (reconcile_lib/textfile.py).

The journal holds two lines for every command the run calls and for its wait on the dpkg lock:
`<action> starts` before it and `<action> took <N>s` after it. A run systemd's timeout killed ends
on the `starts` line of the action it was in, when the time ran out inside a command or the lock
wait (#7490). Python work between commands has no line, so a long gap after a `took` line means
that work.

HOMELAB_ROOT prefixes every path this reads or writes. Only the tests in hosts/lib/tests/ set it.
"""

import fcntl
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import sys
import time
from dataclasses import asdict, dataclass
from pathlib import Path, PurePosixPath

from reconcile_lib import activate, libvirt, lines, textfile, udev, ufw, users
from reconcile_lib.common import ERR, ROOT, run, log, systemctl, timed, under_root

LOCK = ROOT / "var/lib/dpkg/lock-frontend"
MANIFEST = ROOT / "usr/lib/homelab/manifest.json"
APPLIED = ROOT / "var/lib/homelab-reconcile/applied.json"
UNIT_DIR = Path("/usr/lib/systemd/system")
ETC_UNIT_DIR = Path("/etc/systemd/system")
CRON_D = Path("/etc/cron.d")
DROPIN = "50-homelab.conf"
SECRETS_UNIT = "homelab-secrets.service"
SECRETS_JSON = ROOT / "usr/lib/homelab/secrets.json"
CREDSTORE = ROOT / "run/credstore"
RUN_DIR = ROOT / "run/homelab-secrets"
PERSIST_DIR = ROOT / "var/lib/homelab-secrets"
PENDING = RUN_DIR / "pending.json"
PENDING_LOCK = RUN_DIR / "pending.lock"
LOCK_SECONDS = 5  # a holder rewrites a small file; homelab-secrets waits as long

# An upgrade never restarts the run that installed it, whatever the manifest says.
ALWAYS_MANUAL = frozenset({"homelab-updater.service", "homelab-reconcile.service"})
# A template's own name: `claude-land@.service`, with no instance between `@` and the suffix.
TEMPLATE_NAME = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9_.:-]+@\.(service|timer|socket|path)")
UNIT_NAME = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9@_.:-]+\.(service|timer|socket|path)")
PLAIN_NAME = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*")
POLICIES = frozenset({"manual", "windowed"})
# Each arm module has valid(section) and reconcile(section, prev) -> (ok, record | None, drift).
# Users and lines run before the units start: users before the secrets step, whose outputs a new
# user or group can own, and lines before the activations read the files it edits. Libvirt then
# ufw run last, so that ufw's enable applies the sysctl edit already made.
ARMS = {"users": users, "lines": lines, "libvirt": libvirt, "ufw": ufw}
EARLY = ("users", "lines")
SHA256 = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{64}")
# systemctl's exit for a unit that is not loaded: there is nothing to stop.
NOT_LOADED = 5


class ManifestError(Exception):
    """The manifest is missing or malformed, so reconcile acts on nothing."""


def is_homelab(unit: str) -> bool:
    return unit.startswith("homelab-")


# Reading, under the lock.


def read_installed(packages: list[str]) -> dict[str, tuple[str, str]]:
    """Each package's three-letter dpkg status and version; ("", "") for one dpkg lacks."""
    out = run(
        "dpkg-query",
        "-W",
        "-f",
        "${Package} ${db:Status-Abbrev}${Version}\\n",
        *packages,
    ).stdout
    installed = dict.fromkeys(packages, ("", ""))
    for line in out.splitlines():
        name, _, rest = line.partition(" ")
        if name in installed:
            installed[name] = (rest[:3], rest[3:])
    return installed


def load_json(path: Path) -> dict:
    try:
        value = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return value if isinstance(value, dict) else {}


# The legacy entry kinds. Each field is named for its manifest key, which `legacy_key` reads.
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class LegacyPath:
    path: str  # as written: `Path()` drops a trailing `/`, and legacy_key reads it


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class LegacyCron:
    cron: str
    user: str


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class LegacyCronFile:
    cron_file: str


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class LegacyUnit:
    unit: str


Legacy = LegacyPath | LegacyCron | LegacyCronFile | LegacyUnit


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Handoff:
    units: tuple[str, ...]
    legacy: tuple[Legacy, ...]


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Manifest:
    """The manifest, parsed: `units` maps each unit to its policy, None for a unit with none."""

    units: dict[str, str | None]
    enable_links: frozenset[str]
    legacy: tuple[Legacy, ...]
    handoffs: tuple[Handoff, ...]
    unknown_arms: tuple[str, ...]  # listed in `reconcilers`, but not in ARMS
    activations: dict[str, str]
    arms: dict[str, object]
    secrets_digest: str | None


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class AppliedState:
    """The previous run's applied.json. A value of the wrong type reads as absent."""

    units: dict[str, object]
    enable_links: frozenset[str]
    retired: frozenset[str]
    started: dict[str, int]
    files: dict[str, str]
    pending: dict[str, str]
    secrets_digest: str | None
    records: dict[str, dict | None]  # each arm's record, and udev's, by name
    last_ok: dict | None


def parse_applied(raw: dict) -> AppliedState:
    def record(key: str) -> dict | None:
        return raw[key] if isinstance(raw.get(key), dict) else None

    def table(key: str) -> dict:
        return record(key) or {}

    def names(key: str) -> frozenset[str]:
        listed = raw[key] if isinstance(raw.get(key), list) else []
        return frozenset(name for name in listed if isinstance(name, str))

    digest = raw.get("secrets_inputs_sha256")
    return AppliedState(
        units=table("units"),
        enable_links=names("enable_links"),
        retired=names("retired"),
        started={u: when for u, when in table("started").items() if type(when) is int},
        files=table("files"),
        pending=table("pending"),
        secrets_digest=digest if isinstance(digest, str) else None,
        records={name: record(name) for name in (*ARMS, "udev")},
        last_ok=record("last_ok"),
    )


def parse_manifest(text: str) -> Manifest:
    try:
        manifest = json.loads(text)
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise ManifestError(f"{MANIFEST} is not JSON: {exc}") from exc
    if not isinstance(manifest, dict):
        raise ManifestError(f"{MANIFEST} is not an object")
    units = manifest.get("units", {})
    links = manifest.get("enable_links", [])
    legacy = manifest.get("legacy", [])
    reconcilers = manifest.get("reconcilers", [])
    if not isinstance(units, dict) or not all(
        UNIT_NAME.fullmatch(name)
        and isinstance(spec, dict)
        and spec.get("policy") in POLICIES | {None}
        for name, spec in units.items()
    ):
        raise ManifestError(f"{MANIFEST}: units {units!r} is not a map of unit names")
    if not isinstance(links, list) or not all(link in units for link in links):
        raise ManifestError(f"{MANIFEST}: every enable link must name a listed unit")
    entries = tuple(map(parse_legacy, legacy)) if isinstance(legacy, list) else (None,)
    if None in entries:
        raise ManifestError(
            f"{MANIFEST}: legacy {legacy!r} holds an entry of no known kind"
        )
    handoffs = manifest.get("handoffs", [])
    groups = (
        tuple(map(parse_handoff, handoffs)) if isinstance(handoffs, list) else (None,)
    )
    if None in groups:
        raise ManifestError(f"{MANIFEST}: handoffs {handoffs!r} is malformed")
    if not isinstance(reconcilers, list):
        raise ManifestError(f"{MANIFEST}: reconcilers is not a list")
    activations = manifest.get("activations", {})
    if not activate.valid_activations(activations):
        raise ManifestError(f"{MANIFEST}: activations {activations!r} is malformed")
    for name, arm in ARMS.items():
        section = manifest.get(name)
        if (name in reconcilers or section is not None) and not (
            arm.valid(section) and name in reconcilers
        ):
            raise ManifestError(
                f"{MANIFEST}: the {name} section is malformed or unlisted"
            )
    digest = manifest.get("secrets_inputs_sha256")
    if digest is not None and not (
        isinstance(digest, str) and SHA256.fullmatch(digest)
    ):
        raise ManifestError(f"{MANIFEST}: secrets_inputs_sha256 is not a sha256")
    return Manifest(
        units={name: spec.get("policy") for name, spec in units.items()},
        enable_links=frozenset(links),
        legacy=entries,
        handoffs=groups,
        unknown_arms=tuple(name for name in reconcilers if name not in ARMS),
        activations=activations,
        arms={name: manifest.get(name) for name in ARMS},
        secrets_digest=digest,
    )


def parse_legacy(entry: object) -> Legacy | None:
    """The legacy entry as the record of its kind; None for an entry of no known kind."""
    match entry:
        case {"path": str(path)} if len(entry) == 1 and path.startswith("/"):
            return None if ".." in path.split("/") else LegacyPath(path)
        case {"cron": str(cron), "user": str(user)} if len(entry) == 2:
            valid = all(value and "\n" not in value for value in (cron, user))
            return LegacyCron(cron, user) if valid else None
        case {"cron_file": str(name)} if len(entry) == 1 and PLAIN_NAME.fullmatch(name):
            return LegacyCronFile(name)
        case {"unit": str(unit)} if len(entry) == 1 and UNIT_NAME.fullmatch(unit):
            return LegacyUnit(unit)
    return None


def parse_handoff(group: object) -> Handoff | None:
    match group:
        case {"units": list(units), "legacy": list(legacy)} if len(group) == 2 and all(
            isinstance(unit, str) and UNIT_NAME.fullmatch(unit) for unit in units
        ):
            entries = tuple(map(parse_legacy, legacy))
            return None if None in entries else Handoff(tuple(units), entries)
    return None


def wait_for_apt() -> int:
    """`--wait-for-apt`: block until no apt or dpkg frontend holds the lock, then release it."""
    LOCK.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    fd = os.open(LOCK, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT, 0o640)
    timed(
        "wait for apt to release the dpkg lock", lambda: fcntl.lockf(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
    )
    os.close(fd)
    return 0


def read_under_lock(packages: list[str]) -> tuple[str | None, dict, dict]:
    """The manifest text (None when absent), the previous state, and both packages' status.

    Blocks until no apt or dpkg frontend holds the lock, and releases it before returning.
    """
    LOCK.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    fd = os.open(LOCK, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT, 0o640)
    try:
        timed("wait for the dpkg lock", lambda: fcntl.lockf(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX))
        try:
            text = MANIFEST.read_text()
        except FileNotFoundError:
            text = None
        return text, load_json(APPLIED), read_installed(packages)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)  # closing the descriptor releases the lock


# Unit state.


def unit_hash(unit: str) -> dict:
    """The sha256 of the unit's file and of its homelab drop-in; None for one that is absent."""

    def digest(path: Path) -> str | None:
        try:
            return hashlib.sha256(under_root(path).read_bytes()).hexdigest()
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return None

    return {
        "file": digest(UNIT_DIR / unit),
        "dropin": digest(UNIT_DIR / f"{unit}.d" / DROPIN),
    }


def show(names: list[str]) -> list[dict]:
    """The Id, LoadState and ActiveState of each name or glob, one dict per unit, in order.

    An empty list when systemctl refuses the call.
    """
    result = systemctl("show", "-p", "Id,LoadState,ActiveState", *names)
    blocks = result.stdout.split("\n\n") if result.returncode == 0 else []
    return [dict(row.partition("=")[::2] for row in b.split()) for b in blocks]


def unit_states(units: set[str]) -> dict[str, dict]:
    """Each unit's `show` dict, from one call when systemctl answers for every unit.

    The dicts pair with the names by position, because `show` reports an alias by its target's
    Id. One name systemctl refuses fails the whole call, so a refused or short answer is read
    again one unit at a time, and a unit systemctl still refuses gets an empty dict.
    """
    names = sorted(u for u in units if UNIT_NAME.fullmatch(u) and not template_unit(u))
    states = show(names) if names else []
    if len(states) != len(names):
        log("systemctl show did not answer for every unit; asking about each alone")
        states = [next(iter(show([name])), {}) for name in names]
    return dict(zip(names, states, strict=True))


def is_active(unit: str) -> bool:
    return systemctl("is-active", unit).stdout.strip() == "active"


def not_found_homelab_units() -> set[str]:
    """Every loaded `homelab-*` unit whose LoadState is not-found: its file has gone."""
    return {
        props["Id"]
        for props in show(["homelab-*"])
        if props.get("LoadState") == "not-found" and is_homelab(props.get("Id", ""))
    }


def stop(units: list[str]) -> set[str]:
    """Stop each unit, then clear the failed state of every stopped one in one `reset-failed`.

    Return the units whose stop failed. `reset-failed` carries on past a name that is not loaded
    or not failed and then exits 1, so its result is not read.
    """
    stopped, failed = [], set()
    for unit in units:
        result = systemctl("stop", unit)
        if result.returncode in (0, NOT_LOADED):
            log(f"stopped {unit}")
            stopped.append(unit)
        else:
            log(f"stop {unit} failed: {result.stderr.strip()}", ERR)
            failed.add(unit)
    if stopped:  # with no name, reset-failed clears every failed unit on the host
        systemctl("reset-failed", *stopped)
    return failed


# Legacy entries (H3, *The host handoff rule*).


# Each legacy path's owners: step 4 reads them all with one `dpkg -S` before it retires any entry.
OWNERS: dict[str, set[str] | None] = {}


def owned(path: Path) -> bool | None:
    """True when a package owns `path`, False when none does, None when dpkg cannot say."""
    key = str(path)
    owners = OWNERS[key] if key in OWNERS else lines.owners([key])[key]
    return None if owners is None else bool(owners)


def retire_path(path: Path) -> bool:
    """Remove an unowned legacy path, and say whether the entry is retired.

    A directory goes only when it is empty, through `rmdir`. A non-empty one stays and fails
    the entry, so a package lists a directory after the files in it, and reconcile never
    deletes contents nothing named.
    """
    owner = owned(path)
    if owner is None:
        log(f"dpkg -S {path} failed; not removing it", ERR)
        return False
    if owner:
        log(f"{path} is owned by a package; leaving it")
        return True
    target = under_root(path)
    if not os.path.lexists(target):
        return True
    try:
        if target.is_dir() and not target.is_symlink():
            target.rmdir()
        else:
            target.unlink()
    except OSError as exc:
        log(f"cannot remove {path}: {exc}", ERR)
        return False
    log(f"removed {path}")
    return True


def shadow_path(entry: Legacy, units: dict) -> Path | None:
    """The /etc/systemd/system path a legacy entry names, when it can shadow a packaged unit.

    A `{path:}` entry under that directory can, except a `<unit>.d/` directory and a drop-in
    in one whose name is not the packaged drop-in's: neither shadows a packaged file, so
    `retire_path` removes them as unowned files (#6592). A `<unit>.d/50-homelab.conf` shadows
    the packaged drop-in of the same name (#6605). A `{unit:}` entry can when the package
    ships that unit, because an Ansible-era copy of it shadows the packaged one.
    """
    if isinstance(entry, LegacyUnit) and entry.unit in units:
        return ETC_UNIT_DIR / entry.unit
    path = Path(entry.path if isinstance(entry, LegacyPath) else "")
    if ETC_UNIT_DIR not in path.parents:
        return None
    if path.parent == ETC_UNIT_DIR and path.suffix == ".d":
        return None
    dropin_dir = path.parent
    if (
        dropin_dir.parent == ETC_UNIT_DIR
        and dropin_dir.suffix == ".d"
        and path.name != DROPIN
    ):
        return None
    return path


def unit_dir_file(entry: Legacy) -> bool:
    """True for a `{path:}` entry that names a file present under /etc/systemd/system."""
    path = Path(entry.path if isinstance(entry, LegacyPath) else "")
    return ETC_UNIT_DIR in path.parents and os.path.lexists(under_root(path))


def shadowed(path: Path) -> tuple[str, Path | None]:
    """The unit a shadow path configures, and the packaged file it shadows.

    A unit file shadows the packaged unit of its name and a `<unit>.d/50-homelab.conf` the
    packaged drop-in of that unit. A wants link shadows no file: it names its unit only.
    """
    if path.parent == ETC_UNIT_DIR:
        return path.name, UNIT_DIR / path.name
    if path.name == DROPIN and path.parent.parent == ETC_UNIT_DIR:
        unit = path.parent.name.removesuffix(".d")
        return unit, UNIT_DIR / f"{unit}.d" / DROPIN
    return path.name, None


def enable_links_to(path: Path) -> list[Path]:
    """The `*.wants/` and `*.requires/` links in /etc/systemd/system that point at `path`.

    `systemctl enable` links an /etc unit by its absolute path, so a link to the packaged unit,
    or to any other file, is not one of them. A template's instance links carry the instance's
    name, so the match is on the target alone.
    """
    return [
        ETC_UNIT_DIR / link.parent.name / link.name
        for link in sorted(under_root(ETC_UNIT_DIR).glob("*/*"))
        if link.parent.suffix in (".wants", ".requires")
        and link.is_symlink()
        and os.readlink(link) == str(path)
    ]


def hand_off(path: Path) -> tuple[bool, bool] | None:
    """Remove the /etc copy of a unit, or of a unit's drop-in, that the package ships.

    Removing a unit file also removes the enable links in /etc that point at it.

    Returns:
        None when a removal failed, else whether anything was removed and whether the file's
        bytes differ from the packaged file's (a wants link has none, so it never differs,
        and a drop-in the package does not ship always differs).
    """
    target = under_root(path)
    present = os.path.lexists(target)
    packaged = shadowed(path)[1]
    try:
        differs = (
            present
            and packaged is not None
            and target.read_bytes() != under_root(packaged).read_bytes()
        )
    except OSError:
        differs = True
    if not retire_path(path):
        return None
    gone = path.parent == ETC_UNIT_DIR and not os.path.lexists(target)
    links = enable_links_to(path) if gone else []
    if not all(retire_path(link) for link in links):
        return None
    return present or bool(links), differs


def retire_cron(name: str, user: str) -> bool:
    if shutil.which("crontab") is None:
        # No cron package means no crontab holding the entry. Only an absent binary counts:
        # one that is present but will not run fails below, and the entry is retried.
        log(f"crontab is not installed; no {name} to retire")
        return True
    listing = run("crontab", "-l", "-u", user)
    if listing.returncode != 0:
        return "no crontab for" in listing.stderr
    lines = listing.stdout.splitlines(keepends=True)
    marker = f"#Ansible: {name}"
    if not any(line.rstrip("\n") == marker for line in lines):
        return True
    kept, skip = [], False
    for line in lines:
        if skip:
            skip = False
        elif line.rstrip("\n") == marker:
            skip = True
        else:
            kept.append(line)
    if run("crontab", "-u", user, "-", stdin="".join(kept)).returncode != 0:
        log(f"cannot rewrite {user}'s crontab without {name}", ERR)
        return False
    log(f"removed cron {name} from {user}'s crontab")
    return True


def template_unit(unit: str) -> bool:
    """True for a template's own name, `x@.service`, which systemd refuses to start or stop."""
    return TEMPLATE_NAME.fullmatch(unit) is not None


def retire_unit(unit: str) -> bool:
    """Disable, stop and remove an unowned Ansible-era unit; a template is not stopped.

    systemctl refuses a template's own name as missing its instance, and `running_service` has
    already kept the entry while any instance runs.
    """
    path = ETC_UNIT_DIR / unit
    template = template_unit(unit)
    if os.path.lexists(under_root(path)):
        owner = owned(path)
        if owner is not False:
            log(f"{path} is owned by a package, or dpkg cannot say; leaving it")
            return owner is True
        if systemctl("disable", unit).returncode != 0:
            log(f"disable {unit} failed", ERR)
            return False
        if (not template and stop([unit])) or not retire_path(path):
            return False
        return systemctl("daemon-reload").returncode == 0
    return template or not stop([unit])


def legacy_path(entry: LegacyPath | LegacyCronFile | LegacyUnit) -> Path:
    """The file a legacy entry other than a cron retires."""
    match entry:
        case LegacyPath(path):
            return Path(path)
        case LegacyCronFile(name):
            return CRON_D / name
        case LegacyUnit(unit):
            return ETC_UNIT_DIR / unit


def retire(entry: Legacy) -> bool:
    match entry:
        case LegacyCron(cron, user):
            return retire_cron(cron, user)
        case LegacyUnit(unit):
            return retire_unit(unit)
        case LegacyPath() | LegacyCronFile():
            return retire_path(legacy_path(entry))


def legacy_key(entry: Legacy) -> str:
    """The entry as canonical JSON, the form `retired` records it in."""
    return json.dumps(asdict(entry), sort_keys=True)


def replacement_failed(
    handoffs: tuple[Handoff, ...], not_started: set
) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Each legacy key mapped to a unit of its spec that did not start in this run."""
    return {
        legacy_key(entry): unit
        for group in handoffs
        for unit in group.units
        if unit in not_started
        for entry in group.legacy
    }


# The restart queue and the secrets outputs (H4, *The restart contract*).


def read_pending() -> set[str]:
    """The units in pending.json. An absent file is an empty queue, and so is an unreadable one."""
    try:
        queue = json.loads(PENDING.read_text())
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return set()
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        queue = None
    if not isinstance(queue, list):
        log(f"{PENDING} is unreadable; reading it as empty", ERR)
        return set()
    return {unit for unit in queue if isinstance(unit, str)}


def update_pending(add: set[str], remove: set[str]) -> bool:
    """Add and remove units under pending.lock. False when the lock or the write failed."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(
            PENDING_LOCK, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_CLOEXEC, 0o600
        )
    except OSError as exc:
        log(f"cannot open {PENDING_LOCK}: {exc}", ERR)
        return False
    try:
        deadline = time.monotonic() + LOCK_SECONDS
        while True:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                break
            except BlockingIOError:
                if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                    log(f"{PENDING_LOCK} stayed locked for {LOCK_SECONDS}s", ERR)
                    return False
                time.sleep(0.05)
        queue = read_pending()
        wanted = (queue | add) - remove
        if wanted != queue:
            tmp = PENDING.with_name(f".{PENDING.name}.{os.getpid()}")
            tmp.write_text(json.dumps(sorted(wanted)))
            tmp.chmod(0o644)
            os.replace(tmp, PENDING)
        return True
    except OSError as exc:
        log(f"cannot rewrite {PENDING}: {exc}", ERR)
        return False
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class SecretOutput:
    destination: PurePosixPath  # relative, with no empty, `.` or `..` part
    persist: bool
    dependents: frozenset[str]

    def exists(self) -> bool:
        return ((PERSIST_DIR if self.persist else RUN_DIR) / self.destination).is_file()


def parse_output(out: object) -> SecretOutput | None:
    match out:
        case {
            "destination": str(dest),
            "persist": bool(keep),
            "dependents": list(deps),
        }:
            if all(part not in ("", ".", "..") for part in dest.split("/")):
                return SecretOutput(PurePosixPath(dest), keep, frozenset(deps))
    return None


def read_secrets_manifest() -> tuple[list[str], list[SecretOutput]] | None:
    """The credential keys and outputs of secrets.json; None when it is unusable."""
    try:
        manifest = json.loads(SECRETS_JSON.read_text())
        credentials = manifest["credentials"]
        outputs = list(map(parse_output, manifest["outputs"]))
        valid = (
            isinstance(credentials, list)
            and all(isinstance(key, str) for key in credentials)
            and None not in outputs
        )
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        valid = False
    if not valid:
        log(f"{SECRETS_JSON} is unusable", ERR)
        return None
    return credentials, outputs


def missing_outputs(unit: str, outputs: list[SecretOutput]) -> list[str]:
    """The destinations of the outputs that list `unit` as a dependent and do not exist."""
    return [
        str(out.destination)
        for out in outputs
        if unit in out.dependents and not out.exists()
    ]


def secrets_ready(credentials: list[str], outputs: list[SecretOutput]) -> bool:
    """True when homelab-secrets is active and every credential and output exists."""
    return (
        is_active(SECRETS_UNIT)
        and all((CREDSTORE / key).is_file() for key in credentials)
        and all(out.exists() for out in outputs)
    )


def bring_up(
    unit: str, verb: str, outputs: list[SecretOutput], queued: set[str], started: dict
) -> bool:
    """One `reset-failed` and one blocking `start` or `restart` of `unit`, behind the existence gate.

    A unit the gate skips, or whose call fails, joins pending.json, and the result is False.
    A call that succeeds removes the unit from pending.json. `started` records when the call
    was made, whatever its result: a unit that writes a result of its own should have written
    one after that time (#6342).
    """
    missing = missing_outputs(unit, outputs)
    if missing:
        log(f"{verb} {unit} skipped: {', '.join(missing)} does not exist yet")
        update_pending({unit}, set())
        return False
    # DECIDED: one reset-failed per unit, just before its own call, not one for the whole step
    # (#8670). A unit crash-looping on its old config hits its start limit again within a
    # second of a batched reset, and its own restart is then refused.
    systemctl("reset-failed", unit)
    started[unit] = int(time.time())
    if systemctl(verb, unit).returncode != 0:
        log(f"{verb} {unit} failed", ERR)
        update_pending({unit}, set())
        return False
    log(f"{verb} {unit}")
    return update_pending(set(), {unit}) if unit in queued else True


# The run.


def act(manifest: Manifest, prev: AppliedState) -> tuple[bool, dict]:
    """Steps 1 to 5. Return whether every step succeeded, and the advanced per-entry state."""
    ok = True
    units, links = manifest.units, set(manifest.enable_links)
    units_state: dict = {}
    links_state = set(links)
    started = {unit: when for unit, when in prev.started.items() if unit in units}
    digest = manifest.secrets_digest
    manual = ALWAYS_MANUAL | ({SECRETS_UNIT} if digest else set())
    secrets = read_secrets_manifest() if digest else None
    credentials, outputs = secrets or ([], [])
    if digest and secrets is None:
        ok = False
    try:
        if not RUN_DIR.is_dir():
            RUN_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            # Whatever the umask; homelab-secrets corrects an older one.
            RUN_DIR.chmod(0o755)
    except OSError as exc:
        log(f"cannot create {RUN_DIR}: {exc}", ERR)

    # Step 1: stop what the manifest dropped, and every homelab unit whose file has gone.
    dropped = {u for u in set(prev.units) - set(units) if is_homelab(u)}
    unlinked = {
        u for u in prev.enable_links - links if is_homelab(u) and u.endswith(".timer")
    }
    stopping = dropped | unlinked | not_found_homelab_units()
    for unit in stop(sorted(stopping)):
        ok = False
        if unit in prev.units and unit not in units:
            units_state[unit] = prev.units[unit]
        if unit in unlinked:
            links_state.add(unit)

    # Step 1a: the users arm, so an output step 1b writes can name a user this version adds.
    drift, arms = {"legacy": 0}, {}

    def run_arms(names: list[str]) -> bool:
        """Run the named arms. Each record goes in `arms`, and each drift count in `drift`."""
        ok = True
        for name in names:
            arm_ok, record, count = ARMS[name].reconcile(
                manifest.arms[name], prev.records[name]
            )
            ok = ok and arm_ok
            if record is not None:
                arms[name], drift[name] = record, count
        return ok

    ok = run_arms(["users"]) and ok

    # Step 1b: in a package that ships `secrets-unit`, run homelab-secrets when its inputs, its
    # unit file or its activity says the outputs may be stale. A failed run does not stop the rest.
    secrets_state, secrets_ran = None, True
    if digest:
        stale = (
            digest != prev.secrets_digest
            or (
                SECRETS_UNIT in units
                and unit_hash(SECRETS_UNIT) != prev.units.get(SECRETS_UNIT)
            )
            or not is_active(SECRETS_UNIT)
        )
        if stale and systemctl("restart", SECRETS_UNIT).returncode != 0:
            log(
                f"restart {SECRETS_UNIT} failed; carrying on with the outputs that exist",
                ERR,
            )
            secrets_ran = ok = False
        elif stale:
            log(f"restart {SECRETS_UNIT}")
        secrets_state = digest if secrets_ran else prev.secrets_digest

    # Step 1c: the lines arm, so step 3b activates the files it edited.
    ok = run_arms(["lines"]) and ok

    # The restart set, built before step 2: the units whose file or drop-in changed, and the
    # units in pending.json, each only when it is enabled or active. A foreign unit the manifest
    # dropped is walked once more, because its drop-in has gone.
    foreign_dropped = {u for u in prev.units if u not in units and not is_homelab(u)}
    queued = read_pending()
    states = unit_states(set(units) | foreign_dropped | queued | links)
    hashes = {}
    changed = set()
    for unit in sorted((set(units) | foreign_dropped) - dropped):
        hashes[unit] = unit_hash(unit), prev.units.get(unit)
        if hashes[unit][0] != hashes[unit][1] and not left_alone(
            unit, units, links, manual, states
        ):
            changed.add(unit)
    idle = {u for u in queued - changed if left_alone(u, units, links, manual, states)}
    if idle:
        log(f"dropping {', '.join(sorted(idle))} from the queue: nothing to restart")
        ok = update_pending(set(), idle) and ok
    restart_set = sorted(changed | (queued - idle))

    # Step 2: start every enabled unit outside the set that is not active, never a masked or a
    # windowed one. After the first start, a unit is asked again: that start may have pulled it in.
    start_failed, starting = set(), False
    for unit in sorted(links - set(restart_set)):
        if units.get(unit) == "windowed":
            continue
        state = states.get(unit, {})
        if state.get("LoadState") == "masked" or state.get("ActiveState") == "active":
            continue
        if starting and is_active(unit):
            continue
        starting = True
        if not bring_up(unit, "start", outputs, queued, started):
            start_failed.add(unit)
            ok = False

    # Step 3: restart the set. A failed or skipped unit keeps its old hash, so it stays changed.
    failed = {
        u for u in restart_set if not bring_up(u, "restart", outputs, queued, started)
    }
    ok = ok and not failed

    # Step 3b: the `activate:` action of each packaged file whose hash changed. A unit step 3
    # called already gets no second call.
    pending = activate.refresh_pending(prev.pending, manifest.activations)
    called = {unit: unit not in failed for unit in restart_set}
    activated, files_state = activate.activate(
        manifest.activations, prev.files, units, called, pending
    )
    ok = ok and activated
    activation_failed = {unit for unit, result in called.items() if not result} - failed
    if not secrets_ran:
        failed.add(SECRETS_UNIT)
    for unit, (new, old) in hashes.items():
        keep = unit in units
        if unit in failed:
            new, keep = old, True
        if keep:
            units_state[unit] = new

    # Step 4: retire the legacy entries not yet retired. A package that ships `secrets-unit`
    # retires none until every credential and output exists and homelab-secrets is active.
    files = [e for e in manifest.legacy if not isinstance(e, LegacyCron)]
    paths = {str(legacy_path(e)) for e in files if legacy_key(e) not in prev.retired}
    OWNERS.update(lines.owners(sorted(paths)))
    retired = []
    waiting = None
    removed, handed, plain = False, set(), []
    for entry in manifest.legacy:
        key = legacy_key(entry)
        if key not in prev.retired and digest and waiting is None:
            waiting = not (secrets and secrets_ready(credentials, outputs))
        shadow = shadow_path(entry, units)
        # An Ansible-era copy under /etc shadows the packaged unit of the same name, or that
        # unit's packaged drop-in: remove it (one daemon-reload and the restarts follow the
        # loop), whatever the unit's start did, because the copy may be why it failed. With no
        # packaged unit of that name, the file is the only copy, so it stays and counts as drift.
        if shadow and key not in prev.retired and not waiting:
            unit = shadowed(shadow)[0]
            if unit not in units:
                drift["legacy"] += 1
                log(f"keeping {shadow}: the package ships no unit {unit}")
            elif (result := hand_off(shadow)) is None:
                ok = False
            else:
                retired.append(key)
                removed |= result[0]
                if result[1]:
                    handed.add(unit)
        else:
            plain.append(entry)

    # Step 4b: one daemon-reload after every removal under /etc/systemd/system, then each
    # packaged unit whose fragment changed, in the order step 3 uses.
    if removed and systemctl("daemon-reload").returncode != 0:
        log("daemon-reload failed after removing a file under /etc/systemd/system", ERR)
        ok = False
    for unit in sorted(handed):
        fresh = unit_states({unit})
        if not left_alone(unit, units, links, manual, fresh) and not bring_up(
            unit, "restart", outputs, queued, started
        ):
            start_failed.add(unit)
            ok = False

    # Step 4c: the other entries. One stays while a unit of its spec failed to start in this run,
    # step 3b's activation restart included.
    kept_for = replacement_failed(
        manifest.handoffs, start_failed | failed | activation_failed
    )
    removed = False
    asked = {
        e.unit
        for e in plain
        if isinstance(e, LegacyUnit) and legacy_key(e) not in prev.retired
    }
    live = {} if waiting else unit_states(asked)
    for entry in plain:
        key = legacy_key(entry)
        # A running service is never stopped to retire it: the entry stays and counts as drift.
        if key not in prev.retired and not waiting and running_service(entry, live):
            drift["legacy"] += 1
            log(f"keeping {key}: the service is running, so it retires once it is not")
        elif key in prev.retired:
            retired.append(key)
        elif waiting:
            log(f"keeping {key}: the secrets do not all exist yet")
            ok = False
        elif key in kept_for:
            log(f"keeping {key}: {kept_for[key]} failed to start")
        else:
            # A drop-in removed from under /etc/systemd/system stays in force until a reload.
            unit_file = unit_dir_file(entry)
            if retire(entry):
                retired.append(key)
                removed |= unit_file
            else:
                ok = False

    if removed and systemctl("daemon-reload").returncode != 0:
        log("daemon-reload failed after removing a file under /etc/systemd/system", ERR)
        ok = False

    # Step 5: the per-kind reconcilers. The manifest's other kinds are ones this version lacks.
    for name in manifest.unknown_arms:
        log(
            f"the manifest lists reconciler {name!r}, which this version does not have",
            ERR,
        )
        ok = False
    ok = run_arms([name for name in ARMS if name not in EARLY]) and ok
    udev_ok, udev_state = udev.reconcile(prev.records["udev"])
    ok = ok and udev_ok
    if udev_state:
        arms["udev"] = udev_state

    state = {
        "units": units_state,
        "enable_links": sorted(links_state),
        "retired": sorted(retired),
        "files": files_state,
        "pending": pending,
        "started": started,
        "drift": drift,
        **arms,
    }
    if secrets_state:
        state["secrets_inputs_sha256"] = secrets_state
    return ok, state


def running_service(entry: Legacy, states: dict) -> bool:
    """True for a legacy entry that names a service systemd is running or starting.

    For a template service, true while any of its instances is. Any other service is looked up
    in `states`, read after step 4b's restarts, and one systemctl would not answer for counts as
    running, so it stays.
    """
    unit = entry.unit if isinstance(entry, LegacyUnit) else ""
    if not unit.endswith(".service"):
        return False
    if template_unit(unit):
        instances = unit.replace("@.", "@*.", 1)
        listed = systemctl(
            "list-units", "--plain", "--no-legend",
            f"--state={','.join(sorted(activate.RUNNING))}", instances,
        )  # fmt: skip
        return listed.returncode != 0 or bool(listed.stdout.strip())
    active = states.get(unit, {}).get("ActiveState")
    return active is None or active in activate.RUNNING


def left_alone(unit: str, units: dict, links: set, manual: set, states: dict) -> bool:
    """True when reconcile takes the unit's new file, or its queue entry, without acting."""
    state = states.get(unit, {})
    if unit in manual or units.get(unit) in POLICIES:
        return True
    # A masked unit may still have an enable link, so this comes before the next test.
    if state.get("LoadState") == "masked":
        return True
    if is_homelab(unit):
        enabled = unit in links
    else:
        enabled = systemctl("is-enabled", unit).stdout.strip() == "enabled"
    return not (enabled or state.get("ActiveState") == "active")


def write_applied(state: dict) -> None:
    """Step 6: write applied.json atomically."""
    APPLIED.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = APPLIED.with_name(f".{APPLIED.name}.{os.getpid()}")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(state, indent=1, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, APPLIED)


def metrics(host: str) -> int:
    """`--metrics`: rewrite the metrics file from the record, with the flag as it stands now."""
    state = load_json(APPLIED)
    try:
        activations = parse_manifest(MANIFEST.read_text()).activations
    except (OSError, ManifestError):
        activations = None
    if activations is not None:
        pending = parse_applied(state).pending
        state["pending"] = activate.refresh_pending(pending, activations)
    textfile.write(host, state)
    return 0


def main() -> int:
    if sys.argv[1:] == ["--wait-for-apt"]:
        return wait_for_apt()
    host = run("hostname").stdout.strip()
    if sys.argv[1:] == ["--metrics"]:
        return metrics(host)
    packages = ["homelab-updater", f"homelab-{host}"]
    text, prev, installed = read_under_lock(packages)
    applied = parse_applied(prev)
    versions = {name: version for name, (_, version) in installed.items()}

    kept = (
        "units",
        "enable_links",
        "retired",
        "secrets_inputs_sha256",
        "files",
        "pending",
        "started",
        "drift",
        "libvirt",
        "udev",
        "ufw",
        "users",
    )
    state = {k: prev[k] for k in kept if k in prev}
    broken = [name for name, (status, _) in installed.items() if status != "ii "]
    if broken:
        log(f"{', '.join(broken)} is not installed as 'ii '; acting on nothing", ERR)
        ok = False
    else:
        try:
            if text is None:
                raise ManifestError(f"{MANIFEST} does not exist")
            manifest = parse_manifest(text)
        except ManifestError as exc:
            log(f"{exc}; acting on nothing", ERR)
            ok = False
        else:
            ok, state = act(manifest, applied)

    result = {"versions": versions, "ok": ok, "finished": int(time.time())}
    state["result"] = result
    last_ok = result if ok else applied.last_ok
    if last_ok is not None:
        state["last_ok"] = last_ok
    write_applied(state)
    textfile.write(host, state)
    return 0 if ok else 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
